<p class="ns-lede">
This page maps the five layers of a NetScript app and the boundary problem each one solves. Read
the sections in order, then follow each one into the detailed documentation.
</p>

## 1. Contracts

A contract is the source of truth for an operation's input and output. It can describe behavior as
well as data: `checkout.start` is as natural a boundary as a list or update operation. A service
implements the oRPC contract, `@netscript/sdk` derives clients and query helpers from the same
contract map, and the service runtime generates OpenAPI from the contract router. The TypeScript
caller and the HTTP description share one definition instead of two hand-written shapes.

[Contracts and type flow](/explanation/contracts/) covers the schemas and router mechanics.

## 2. Services

A service runs a contract at request time. `defineService()` turns a contract router into a running
Hono and oRPC service. The preset wires in request logging, OpenAPI generation, a Scalar docs UI,
RPC, health endpoints, and service-info metadata. SDK clients resolve service URLs lazily from
Aspire-injected environment values, so callers don't pin a local port into application code.

A service owns the request boundary. Work that must resume after the process exits belongs in a
durable runtime, not in a longer request handler. See [Services & SDK](/services-sdk/) and
[service discovery](/services-sdk/how-to/discover-services/).

## 3. Plugins

Services handle requests; plugins handle work that outlives one. The host starts empty, and plugins
add its runtime capabilities. A plugin manifest is validated data that declares services,
background processors, schemas, topics, configuration, and telemetry contributions. The CLI turns
those declarations into static workspace wiring.

A plugin is a packaging unit, not a runtime unit: Aspire can run a plugin's API and its background
processors as separate resources. Workers, sagas, triggers, and streams are the durable examples.
[The plugin-system explanation](/explanation/plugin-system/) covers the manifest and registry
mechanics.

## 4. The web layer

The web layer carries the contract to the browser. `@netscript/fresh` builds on Fresh 2 and Preact.
Typed route contracts and `definePage()` connect paths, search parameters, metadata, handlers, data
layers, and forms. A server page can call the contract-derived SDK, hydrate TanStack Query state,
and hand interactive work to islands. Routes that need no island ship no island JavaScript.

[The web-layer overview](/web-layer/) has the complete model. The focused guides cover
[server rendering](/web-layer/server/), [page builders](/web-layer/builders/), and
[queries and islands](/web-layer/query/).

## 5. Observability

{{ comp.diagram({ src: "/assets/diagrams/aspire-resource-graph.svg", alt: "An Aspire AppHost starts Postgres, Redis, and an orders plugin's API, worker, and saga runner; the dashboard observes each resource, while the three processes share database and cache connections.", caption: "One plugin can become several observed resources. Aspire starts its API and background processors alongside their shared infrastructure." }) }}

The diagram shows one plugin. At workspace scale, the generated AppHost also composes apps,
services, plugin processors, databases, and cache resources, then injects service-discovery and
OTLP configuration into their processes. The Aspire dashboard reads health, logs, traces, and
metrics from that running graph. Trace context carried by SDK calls keeps client and service spans
connected.

Aspire is the default local orchestration layer, not part of every package API. Without it, the
Deno packages still work, but resource startup, discovery values, and telemetry wiring become the
application's responsibility. [Orchestration & runtime](/orchestration-runtime/),
[Observability](/observability/), and [the Aspire boundary](/explanation/aspire/) cover the
mechanics.

